72a62e03-ddf9-557c-8ed7-f6d22f5d752f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088868 #edb/ref 1064 "Adding a value to $y=2^x$ will **translate** (shift) the exponential function up or down the $y$-axis. \n\nFor instance, adding $3$ to $y=2^x$ will move each point on the exponential function $3$ units up.\n\n![](images/c8/c89fd5fdcdc3773143a5f14ecb20f5bd2917bce20ece18a7dc70631bf358a0e8.png)\n\nNote the following points:\n\n- The $y$-intercept $(0,1)$ has moved up $3$ units to the point $(0,4).$\n\n- The horizontal asymptote $y=0$ has moved up $3$ units to $y=3.$\n\nAs a second example, let's consider the function $y=2^x-3.$ In this case, the graph of $y=2^x$ is shifted *down* by $3$ units.\n\n![](images/84/84987b3e9138a2e7e8afe70a7aa28a1cd81e081033aa2a766773e63cb8f41da5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088868 #edb/ref 1065 1542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088868 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c549a4f-8132-5b94-bae2-990ac8abaf08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088868 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088869 #edb/ref 1064 "When an exponential growth function is vertically shifted *and* stretched, we apply the stretch first and the shift second.\n\nFor example, to plot the function $y=2(4^x)+3,$ we start by plotting the graph of $y=2(4^x).$ Stretching $y=4^x$ by a factor of $2$ moves the $y$-intercept from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5d/5d6bdd7aa3290567fc6a64747ea605970fcb1d0f05ba06907134e7fc0a3e290b.png)\n\nThen, to plot the graph of $y=2(4^x)+3,$ we take the graph of $y=2(4^x)$ and shift it $3$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=3.$\n\n![](images/19/191a51b0b4b7da7c6744240f36786d55b8b525356b8d0300750fb48af5a2f06a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088869 #edb/ref 1065 1543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088869 #edb/ref 1066 #uui