 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={7}^{x}+4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63caaf01-44b4-5dc6-a569-9e3a3a5c8859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390514 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={7}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/cd/cd8226319b26b3fef6db6e98efc187b1c474c91bc0252e424151791fda373b95.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={7}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/85/85cc1403ab74e2df97669a7c3363d1abddf6d093c358e888a69b655f1ca7450a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186390520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390515 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe0ebeea-e82f-58d4-a5b0-0f856e91026c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390516 #edb/ref 1080 "images/61/61cd6304483770e91658f37cd8ac3dbf57c58f25c9a631ab45a56cd706d381ee.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390516 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390516 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1db17739-2def-5e37-af22-164593bd5829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390517 #edb/ref 1080 "images/31/31f0d95293939e141e0ebff2aaad6223a9a82188e892441906ae09c530919b1d.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9904a233-df69-591c-90af-01e22fb617bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390518 #edb/ref 1080 "images/df/dfb46737087c1196ce079607220107a88857d6ca139d0d5c53b181795fe1344a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "acd865cc-8ca0-5df0-b0c5-c2cd29210f44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390519 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ee/eef682605cf7486a9d8fa23cd508705e70443622164eeb2c253ca342bbce92cb.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ddc2a988-d900-5cd3-a9a0-9a6ecf303b6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390520 #edb/ref 1080 "images/bc/bce2bc405efee4fc0fc0f8cd53a3f230fe407f1232c98237ced4ce64743a8499.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fffb642d-e2f2-5134-92b3-6bde0b2a9298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513300 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513251 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c5/c5d4946b7a2c5953b4ce4c12dcf11273bed5e488e122b90f61dad802ffd81f7d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({6}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "205d2e74-e2d5-5e18-a26f-6b9a011d134a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513251 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({6}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5,$ so $c=2.5.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({6}^{x})+2.5.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ Reading the calibrated grid, the marked point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+2.5 \\\\ 6 & =k({6}^{0})+2.5 \\\\ 6 & =k(1)+2.5 \\\\ 6 & =k+2.5 \\\\ 3.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3.5({6}^{x})+2.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513257 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513252 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/3241259f78335a4b6589025e4f033ae92ab7ab82edec137e1dcb9531579fdc8a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "233d6bb5-1ddb-5d78-a70c-c0bc5382721e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513252 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ so $c=-3.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-3.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-3 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-3 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-3 \\\\ 2 & =k-3 \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5$ and $c=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513312 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513255 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513255 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/87/87a7011de202f4e761be0684e15294fb736ebdf1bd284b4625d0475a2788b5b3.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({3}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b8005c7-3a74-5b35-a129-e65f3f4397cc" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513255 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({3}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4,$ so $c=-4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({3}^{x})-4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({3}^{x})-4 \\\\ -2 & =k({3}^{0})-4 \\\\ -2 & =k(1)-4 \\\\ -2 & =k-4 \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=2$ and $c=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513256 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513281 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513256 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513256 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/12/12b29bcde388b31bb0058dba21d728dbc058295e5d5fa1cf4468118918246b33.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({3}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d43ec8c-6bf0-5541-ae55-e7217a0478fa" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513256 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({3}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.75,$ so $c=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({3}^{x})+0.75.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ Reading the calibrated grid, the marked point on the graph is $(0,5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({3}^{x})+0.75 \\\\ 5 & =k({3}^{0})+0.75 \\\\ 5 & =k(1)+0.75 \\\\ 5 & =k+0.75 \\\\ 4.25 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=4.25({3}^{x})+0.75.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513287 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513261 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82bc393fda58a2ef7cc1682228083d2d6b85e888b3b32ccf8b210b80eeca2171.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({4}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a51df2a-1824-53b2-bd59-7763e88d0cd5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513261 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({4}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({4}^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({4}^{x})+4 \\\\ 6.5 & =k({4}^{0})+4 \\\\ 6.5 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 6.5 & =k+4 \\\\ 2.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=2.5({4}^{x})+4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513266 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513243 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513266 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513266 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f94ee013453893203d2fc5aae2b8ad7965e7fce3d76d16ad62347e3ef72bfb72.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({6}^{x})+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "531a3de7-82f1-5d81-b704-c24a46a8612a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513266 #edb/ref 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({6}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5,$ so $c=2.5.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({6}^{x})+2.5.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ Reading the calibrated grid, the marked point on the graph is $(0,7),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=7$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({6}^{x})+2.5 \\\\ 7 & =k({6}^{0})+2.5 \\\\ 7 & =k(1)+2.5 \\\\ 7 & =k+2.5 \\\\ 4.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=4.5({6}^{x})+2.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513259 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513299 #edb/ref 