045443] [17592186513405 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=5({6}^{x})-8?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513405 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db5b0c8b-dd31-584b-8bb7-f42a116b6bda" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513405 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1b/1b936cf103a607a6b26a060ba747717062698ab005910923f36d54f1d002f119.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=5({6}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/85/85d83e3770cd6995bb95be06df90379f89777d0e322d4029c698e4fe2e3e0848.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=5({6}^{x})-8.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $8$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,5)$ to $(0,-3),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-8.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a958c436a5a31af4676b1dd2747d29055dd957dbb04468fa679b5ba62407e56.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513408 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513392 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513408 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513408 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=8({1.5}^{x})+4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8eb9941-3ad1-5843-95c0-9bfc608a7ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513408 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={1.5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/8e/8eef43ea7539e0932505e1a59487ca0ce87dc63c463c8a7b51265aa5286db6d0.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=8({1.5}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $8.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,8),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8bcef6718ccb06bfbca4b65b41989511107104dede1328f1031ff8b7426241.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=8({1.5}^{x})+4.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,8)$ to $(0,12),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/ae/aed02a04b49c9a428422c53894df87391e6d8f882e11935a659ac714ff63d241.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513374 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513418 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=6.5({3}^{x})-0.5?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb0cfba4-eeeb-5c5b-b7db-97b99d0eccb2" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513418 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={3}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/6a/6a7938d23d1de5e91d734afd9a930d1d8cc1f82407dd484177d3f7852a27a915.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=6.5({3}^{x}).$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $6.5.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,6.5),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/8f/8f8c99a7d9b4ea251c25da35ffce56ca1a8e94225dcab7075bf687e192ad0a09.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=6.5({3}^{x})-0.5.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $0.5$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,6.5)$ to $(0,6),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-0.5.$\n\n![](images/74/748d7970553a8d054c415882a443e61bcd2f858da92079d537bc3574e36c28b9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513395 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513419 #edb/ref 1071 "For the curve $y=6({10}^{x})-8,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff4b88d8-da28-5f54-8746-3c0f00566a0c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513419 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=6({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=6\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=6-8=-2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6({10}^{0})-8 \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-8 \\\\  & =6-8 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=6({10}^{x})-8,$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513420 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186087823.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513420 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "35bd8dd3-54c5-5fe0-8635-7b694fdfe873" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513430 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513442 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{3})}^{x}-\\frac{7}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58a209a8-a2e1-5a80-8adb-5059f728c0cf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513442 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{3})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/59/59f4e7a3dc5b0b263a9580604c82f3e0fc177de6e91414e95616a4840d641056.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{3})}^{x}-\\frac{7}{2},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{3})}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{7}{2}$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,- \\frac{5}{2}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=- \\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n![](images/04/04e3164bee7ad33d4fea543b9ed815091af653f891c6b11e52521e4a5da2139b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513435 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513446 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={5}^{x}-3.25?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b5aa3c3-a81b-5d2d-a667-2b5ab1d8cd37" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513446 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/b4/b443f191d33e60946ec200967d3372bdaf6670caca162e6d6